# Length and density: too long and repetitive

First Metropolitan Bank is filing this SAR on customer Patricia Reynolds, age 68, of Phoenix, Arizona, regarding suspected elder financial exploitation and possible romance-scam-related fraud involving personal checking account 48192076. Reynolds opened the account on March 18, 2017. At account opening, Reynolds identified herself as a retired elementary school teacher receiving pension and Social Security income. The account has historically been used for pension deposits, Social Security deposits, household expenses, utilities, insurance payments, retail purchases, credit card payments, charitable donations, and other routine personal expenditures.

Reynolds receives approximately $4,100 per month in combined pension and Social Security income. Review of account activity from January 2024 through April 2026 showed no outgoing international wires, no cryptocurrency-related transactions, and no domestic wires exceeding $5,000. Historical activity was generally stable and consistent with the customer’s stated retired status and personal use of the account.

Reynolds’s routine monthly expenses include an approximately $1,460 mortgage payment, electric utility payments generally ranging from $110 to $240, water and municipal utility payments ranging from approximately $55 to $90, recurring insurance payments totaling approximately $320 per month, cellular telephone payments ranging from $68 to $82, grocery purchases, gasoline purchases, pharmacy transactions, and ordinary retail spending.

Historical review also showed recurring charitable donations ranging from $25 to $100 to several nonprofit organizations. Reynolds periodically made personal checks to family members in amounts generally below $500 and had occasionally transferred amounts between $1,000 and $2,500 to a savings account held in her own name. None of this historical activity involved international beneficiaries, cryptocurrency platforms, large cash withdrawals, or recently established third-party accounts.

Beginning May 12, 2026, Reynolds’s transaction behavior changed materially.

Between May 12, 2026, and August 18, 2026, Reynolds initiated seven outgoing wire transfers totaling $184,750 to five beneficiaries located in the United States, Hong Kong, and Singapore. Individual transfers ranged from $18,500 to $36,000.

On May 12, 2026, Reynolds initiated a $22,500 domestic wire to Horizon Management Services LLC at a financial institution in New York.

On May 29, 2026, Reynolds initiated a $27,000 international wire to Bright Ocean Import Ltd. at a financial institution in Hong Kong.

On June 16, 2026, Reynolds initiated a $18,500 domestic wire to Granite Peak Consulting LLC at a financial institution in Florida.

On July 2, 2026, Reynolds initiated a $31,250 international wire to Bright Ocean Import Ltd. at the same Hong Kong financial institution used for the May 29 transfer.

On July 20, 2026, Reynolds initiated a $24,500 international wire to East Meridian Trading Pte. Ltd. at a financial institution in Singapore.

On August 4, 2026, Reynolds initiated a $25,000 domestic wire to Sterling Administrative Solutions LLC at a financial institution in California.

On August 18, 2026, Reynolds initiated a $36,000 international wire to East Meridian Trading Pte. Ltd. in Singapore.

The seven wires totaled $184,750. Reynolds had no international wire activity during at least the preceding 28 months reviewed by First Metropolitan Bank, and she had not previously made domestic wire transfers of similar size.

Funding for the wires came primarily from Reynolds’s existing savings and from the liquidation of investment assets.

On May 8, 2026, Reynolds transferred $40,000 from a savings account in her own name into checking account 48192076. Four days later, she sent the $22,500 wire to Horizon Management Services LLC.

On May 26, 2026, Reynolds received a $35,000 ACH credit from an investment brokerage account in her own name. Three days later, she sent $27,000 to Bright Ocean Import Ltd.

On June 13, 2026, Reynolds received a $25,000 transfer from her savings account. On June 16, she wired $18,500 to Granite Peak Consulting LLC.

On June 29, 2026, Reynolds received a $42,000 ACH credit from the same investment brokerage. On July 2, she wired $31,250 to Bright Ocean Import Ltd.

Additional transfers from Reynolds’s savings and investment accounts preceded the July 20, August 4, and August 18 wire transactions.

The movement of funds from Reynolds’s savings and investments into her checking account represented a significant change from historical behavior. Prior to May 2026, Reynolds generally maintained stable balances in her savings and brokerage relationships and did not routinely liquidate investments to fund payments to third parties.

First Metropolitan Bank first questioned Reynolds about the activity on May 29, 2026, when she visited a Phoenix branch to initiate the $27,000 wire to Bright Ocean Import Ltd. Reynolds told the branch employee that she was sending money to assist a friend with an overseas business matter.

The branch employee asked how Reynolds knew the beneficiary. Reynolds stated that she had met an individual named “Michael” online approximately four months earlier and that Michael worked internationally as an engineer and business consultant.

Reynolds said Michael had encountered temporary financial problems related to an overseas contract and needed assistance paying business expenses. Reynolds stated that Michael intended to repay her after his contract was completed.

The employee asked Reynolds whether she had met Michael in person. Reynolds stated that she had not. She reported communicating with him through text messages, email, and an online messaging application.

The employee warned Reynolds that individuals sometimes use online romantic or personal relationships to persuade victims to send money and asked whether anyone had instructed her to conceal the purpose of the transfer. Reynolds stated that nobody had asked her to lie and that she trusted Michael.

The May 29 wire was processed after Reynolds confirmed that she understood the warning and wished to proceed.

On June 16, 2026, Reynolds returned to a First Metropolitan Bank branch to initiate the $18,500 wire to Granite Peak Consulting LLC. A branch employee again questioned the payment because of the recent change in Reynolds’s account activity.

Reynolds stated that the payment was connected to Michael’s business project and represented a required consulting fee. She did not provide a contract, invoice, engagement letter, or other documentation showing why Granite Peak Consulting LLC was owed the funds.

Reynolds stated that Michael had sent her instructions identifying the account and amount to be paid.

First Metropolitan Bank personnel reminded Reynolds of the prior fraud warning. Reynolds stated that she understood the bank’s concern but believed Michael was legitimate.

On July 2, 2026, Reynolds initiated the second wire to Bright Ocean Import Ltd., this time for $31,250. Bank personnel again discussed the transaction with Reynolds.

Reynolds stated that the payment was required to release equipment being held by customs authorities in connection with Michael’s project. She did not provide customs documentation, shipping records, invoices, bills of lading, or government correspondence supporting the stated explanation.

Reynolds again stated that she had not met Michael in person but expected to meet him after the project was completed.

First Metropolitan Bank’s fraud department conducted an enhanced review of Reynolds’s account on July 6, 2026.

The review confirmed that Reynolds had historically used the account for ordinary personal expenses and retirement income. The review did not identify prior commercial activity, international business dealings, consulting arrangements, import or export activity, or other transactions that would explain the new wire behavior.

The enhanced review also confirmed that Reynolds’s historical spending consisted largely of predictable personal expenses. For example, she had recurring payments to grocery stores, pharmacies, utility providers, insurance companies, a local home-improvement retailer, streaming services, restaurants, and other merchants in the Phoenix metropolitan area.

The review identified a recurring $14.99 streaming subscription, a $9.99 cloud-storage subscription, monthly purchases at two local grocery chains, quarterly pest-control payments, periodic veterinary expenses, and several purchases from a home-goods retailer. These transactions were consistent with Reynolds’s historical use of the account and did not appear connected to the suspicious wire activity.

The review also identified several ordinary debit card purchases made while Reynolds was traveling within Arizona during the review period. These included gasoline purchases in Flagstaff, a hotel charge in Sedona, restaurant purchases, and a purchase at a national pharmacy chain. First Metropolitan Bank identified no reason to believe these transactions were connected to the wire activity.

The bank’s review of these routine transactions reinforced that the wire activity represented a significant departure from Reynolds’s historical account use.

On July 10, 2026, First Metropolitan Bank contacted Reynolds by telephone and asked her to provide additional information regarding Michael and the purpose of the transfers.

Reynolds stated that Michael’s full name was Michael Andrews and that he was approximately 61 years old. She stated that he was originally from the United States but was currently working overseas. Reynolds did not provide a verifiable employer, permanent business address, or corporate affiliation for Michael.

Reynolds said Michael had told her that he was involved in an engineering project requiring purchases of equipment and payment of taxes, customs fees, consulting charges, and administrative costs.

Reynolds provided screenshots of several messages from Michael. The messages contained affectionate language and references to a future relationship between Michael and Reynolds. The messages also contained instructions concerning payments.

In one message, Michael told Reynolds that a payment needed to be made quickly or equipment would be seized. In another, he told her that a business associate would provide banking information for a required fee. In a separate message, Michael stated that he would repay Reynolds “everything plus extra” once his project funds were released.

The messages reviewed by the bank did not contain independent documentation establishing the existence of the engineering project.

First Metropolitan Bank conducted open-source research concerning the beneficiaries.

Horizon Management Services LLC was identified as an active New York limited liability company formed in February 2026. Its public filing listed a commercial registered-agent address. First Metropolitan Bank identified no apparent relationship between Reynolds and the company.

Granite Peak Consulting LLC was identified as an active Florida limited liability company formed in March 2026. Its registered address was associated with a virtual office provider. First Metropolitan Bank identified no apparent relationship between Reynolds and the company.

Sterling Administrative Solutions LLC was identified as a California company formed in April 2026. The company’s public information did not identify any relationship to Reynolds.

Bright Ocean Import Ltd. appeared in publicly available commercial records as a Hong Kong entity. East Meridian Trading Pte. Ltd. appeared in public corporate information as a Singapore entity. The bank did not identify information establishing a relationship between Reynolds and either company.

No sanctions matches were identified for Reynolds or the named beneficiaries during the bank’s review.

On July 20, 2026, Reynolds initiated the $24,500 wire to East Meridian Trading Pte. Ltd. She told bank personnel the payment related to taxes associated with Michael’s business project.

Bank personnel again warned Reynolds that the activity displayed characteristics commonly associated with scams involving online relationships and requests for money. Reynolds stated that she understood but wished to proceed.

On August 4, 2026, Reynolds initiated a $25,000 wire to Sterling Administrative Solutions LLC. Reynolds stated that this payment represented an administrative fee required before Michael could receive funds owed to him.

The bank again requested documentation. Reynolds provided a one-page document titled “Administrative Release Certificate” that purported to show a $25,000 fee. The document contained no government agency name, regulatory authority, mailing address, telephone number, or independently verifiable contact information.

Bank personnel were unable to independently verify the document through the information it contained.

On August 12, 2026, First Metropolitan Bank received a telephone call from Reynolds’s adult daughter, Karen Reynolds. Karen stated that she was concerned her mother was being financially exploited by an individual she had met online.

Karen stated that Reynolds had told family members she was planning to marry Michael after he returned to the United States. Karen stated that family members had never spoken directly with Michael and had been unable to independently verify his identity.

Karen was not an owner or authorized signer on Reynolds’s account. First Metropolitan Bank did not disclose account information to Karen but documented the information she provided as part of its ongoing review.

On August 18, 2026, Reynolds attempted to initiate a $36,000 wire to East Meridian Trading Pte. Ltd.

During the transaction, bank personnel again discussed the prior fraud warnings with Reynolds and explained that the bank remained concerned she might be the victim of a scam.

Reynolds stated that Michael had told her the $36,000 payment would be the final amount needed before his funds were released. She stated that she remained confident Michael would repay her.

The transaction was escalated for additional review.

First Metropolitan Bank considers the activity suspicious because Reynolds, a retired customer with no history of international business activity, sent $184,750 through seven wires to five previously unrelated domestic and foreign beneficiaries after developing an online relationship with an individual she had never met in person.

The activity was funded primarily through transfers from Reynolds’s savings and liquidation of investment assets and represented a substantial deviation from her historical account activity.

Reynolds repeatedly stated that the funds were being sent for business expenses associated with Michael, including consulting fees, customs-related expenses, taxes, administrative fees, and other costs. Reynolds was unable to provide independently verifiable documentation establishing the underlying business project, her relationship to the beneficiary companies, or a legitimate reason she was responsible for paying the expenses.

The pattern is consistent with suspected elder financial exploitation and possible romance-scam activity in which a victim is persuaded by an online contact to send increasing amounts of money to third-party beneficiaries based on purported emergencies, business expenses, fees, or promises of future repayment.

First Metropolitan Bank has not established Michael Andrews’s true identity, whether the named beneficiary companies are knowingly participating in fraudulent activity, or whether all documentation provided to Reynolds was fraudulent.

First Metropolitan Bank restricted Reynolds’s ability to initiate additional outgoing wires on August 18, 2026 and referred the account for enhanced review. The account remains open.

First Metropolitan Bank maintains account statements, wire records, customer communications, screenshots provided by Reynolds, transaction records, customer due diligence information, public-record research, and other supporting documentation and will make those records available to appropriate authorities upon request.
